WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.880
In terms of human ancestry, genomic data has
helped reinforce a theory that humans have

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.500
spread out across the globe from Africa in
the recent past.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.830
This has led to a large number of variants
that are shared across the globe and fluctuate

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.849
in only subtle levels.

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:20.321
Those fluctuations though are very geographic
in their nature, such that individuals that

00:00:20.321 --> 00:00:24.749
are closer together have much more similar
allele frequencies.

00:00:24.749 --> 00:00:29.389
That can be used to reconstruct population
movements and even individual-level ancestry

00:00:29.389 --> 00:00:32.940
of where someone's grandparents or great-grandparents
come from.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:38.089
The other major signal that we've been learning
about using genomic data is about how the

00:00:38.089 --> 00:00:42.860
human population has been growing in many
places throughout the globe.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:49.740
That growth has been tied with the appearance
of many new rare variants that are geographically localized.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:56.540
And an exciting area is using those rare variants
to uncover very recent time period migration patterns.

